redact credentials out of argv, harden uninstall orders, atomic apm swap, TLS for remote databases

Process command lines were shipped to the backend verbatim. Measured on a live
fleet: a service was passing a database URL with its password in argv, and that
password had been sitting in the telemetry store for weeks. Command lines now
pass through one shared redaction set (internal/redact) before they leave the
host, and the log tailer shares it instead of keeping a weaker copy.

The old log pattern cut values at the first space, so password="two words"
leaked its tail; the shared set understands quoted values, --flag forms, env
assignments and PEM headers, and keeps url credentials readable as
scheme://user:***@host. Two rules were dropped after measuring them against
the whole fleet: a bare -p matched -pidfile and -proto, and -u user:pass
matched uid:gid, so both are now gated on the binary that actually uses them
(mysql family, curl). Tests lock every foreign flag that was mangled.

Uninstall orders carried an issue time that nothing checked. An order is now
refused when it is older than a day or when its issue time was already seen,
so a captured envelope is not a licence to wipe the host later.

An APM bundle refresh deleted the applied directory before renaming the new one
into place. An active prepend reads that path on every request, so the deletion
window is a window of fatals across every site on the host. The swap now moves
the old tree aside and restores it if the rename fails.

Remote database probes sent stored credentials in the clear (postgres
sslmode=disable, mssql encrypt=disable, mysql and redis without TLS at all).
Loopback and unix sockets stay plaintext; a remote target now requires verified
TLS unless the signed definition asks for an exception. No definition in the
catalog points at a remote database today, so this is preventive.

service install pointed launchd or SCM at whatever binary was running, so an
administrator running it from a writable directory created a future root or
SYSTEM execution path. The path is now validated first.

The manual release fallback wrote no signed manifest, and agents fail closed
without one: a channel published that way froze updates while the version file
promised a new release. It now writes and signs the manifest and the versioned
archive, and the version bump stays last so a failure leaves the old channel.
